body:
This PR changes the internal API for segments in Geometry2D/3D to take the segment points directly as parameters, instead of taking a pointer to an array of 2 or more Vector2s. The main motivation behind this PR is to make the internal API consistent with GDExtension and GDScript, so I can write C++ code that targets both. However, this honestly makes the internal code clearer too, and may even have slight performance improvements (I haven't benchmarked it).

A lot of code was needlessly making local copies of data. For example, in `gradient_texture.cpp`, before:

```cpp
Vector2 segment[2];
segment[0] = fill_from;
segment[1] = fill_to;
Vector2 closest = Geometry2D::get_closest_point_to_segment_uncapped(pos, &segment[0]);
```

After this PR:

```cpp
const Vector2 closest = Geometry2D::get_closest_point_to_segment_uncapped(pos, fill_from, fill_to);
```

In most cases, there wasn't already an array ready to go, one had to be constructed, all to conform to this API.

Some code has been made clearer with this PR. Some examples:

<details>

This code in WorldBoundaryShape2D used nested arrays of Vector2s and gave a hint at some kind of iteration:

```cpp
bool WorldBoundaryShape2D::_edit_is_selected_on_click(const Point2 &p_point, double p_tolerance) const {
	Vector2 point = distance * normal;
	Vector2 l[2][2] = { { point - normal.orthogonal() * 100, point + normal.orthogonal() * 100 }, { point, point + normal * 30 } };

	for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
		Vector2 closest = Geometry2D::get_closest_point_to_segment(p_point, l[i]);
		if (p_point.distance_to(closest) < p_tolerance) {
			return true;
		}
	}

	return false;
}
```

Now it's much clearer, including explicitly marking which code is checking the orthogonal and normal segments. It's a bit longer because it does not have all points on a single line anymore and has the closest point and distance checks repeated, but I think it's better overall:

```cpp
bool WorldBoundaryShape2D::_edit_is_selected_on_click(const Point2 &p_point, double p_tolerance) const {
	const Vector2 shape_center = distance * normal;
	// Orthogonal part of the shape editor gizmo (the flat line).
	const Vector2 ortho_segment_a = shape_center - normal.orthogonal() * 100;
	const Vector2 ortho_segment_b = shape_center + normal.orthogonal() * 100;
	const Vector2 ortho_closest = Geometry2D::get_closest_point_to_segment(p_point, ortho_segment_a, ortho_segment_b);
	if (p_point.distance_to(ortho_closest) < p_tolerance) {
		return true;
	}
	// Normal part of the shape editor gizmo (the arrow).
	const Vector2 normal_segment_a = shape_center;
	const Vector2 normal_segment_b = shape_center + normal * 30;
	const Vector2 normal_closest = Geometry2D::get_closest_point_to_segment(p_point, normal_segment_a, normal_segment_b);
	if (p_point.distance_to(normal_closest) < p_tolerance) {
		return true;
	}
	return false;
}
```

This code in `godot_shape_3d.cpp` was setting a value in a Vector3 in an array. Before:

```cpp
//check segments
real_t min_distance = 1e20;
Vector3 closest_vertex = half_extents * p_point.sign();
Vector3 s[2] = {
	closest_vertex,
	closest_vertex
};

for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
	s[1] = closest_vertex;
	s[1][i] = -s[1][i]; //edge

	Vector3 closest_edge = Geometry3D::get_closest_point_to_segment(p_point, s);

	real_t d = p_point.distance_to(closest_edge);
	if (d < min_distance) {
		min_point = closest_edge;
		min_distance = d;
	}
}
```

Now it avoids copying `closest_vertex` for the first segment point and remakes `segment_b` each time, making the flow of data clearer. After:

```cpp
//check segments
real_t min_distance = 1e20;
const Vector3 closest_vertex = half_extents * p_point.sign();
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
	Vector3 segment_b = closest_vertex;
	segment_b[i] = -segment_b[i]; //edge

	const Vector3 closest_edge = Geometry3D::get_closest_point_to_segment(p_point, closest_vertex, segment_b);

	const real_t d = p_point.distance_to(closest_edge);
	if (d < min_distance) {
		min_point = closest_edge;
		min_distance = d;
	}
}
```

</details>

Aside from needless copying, on a technical level, references are syntax sugar for pointers. This means that in the worst case, where an array already exists and now we need to pass `something[0]` and `something[1]` in place of `something`, or in a loop `something[i]` and `something[i + 1]` in place of `&something[i]`, all we are really doing is moving the addition of `+ 1` of the pointer out of the function, so it shouldn't have hardly any performance difference. In the common cases, it will probably be (slightly!) faster this way, but again I haven't benchmarked.

In a few places, this PR also adds `const`, renames some local variables, uses `real_t`, and adds some comments.

The big thing is now the API is designed in a way that is more compatible with how most other APIs in the engine are done, making things easier for call sites since they don't need to make a 2-element array, and making the internal API more like the exposed API.
